.SS "template<uint32_t dim, template< uint32_t > class CELL> void \fBcsmp::ModelSubDomain\fP< dim, CELL >::AssignCellCharacteristicsTo (const char * characteristic, const char * var)"

.PP
characteristics like 'length', 'area', 'volume' , 'aspect ratio', 'inner radius' are assigned to user-defined variable \fBAssignCellCharacteristicsTo()\fP allows to assign a number of \fBElement\fP characteristics as identified by strings (second argument) to scalar physical variables\&. These characteristics are:

.PP
"volume" (3D) "inner radius" "aspect ratio" (longest / shortest segment)
.SH "Input Arguments"
.PP
The cell characteristic indicated by the first string argument is assigned to a property of the user's choice specified by the second method argument and as identified by its name in the variable database\&.
.SH "Implementation"
.PP
The method was implemented mainly for two-dimensional calculations\&. Therefore, height and width only work in two-dimensional calculations\&. The characteristics are obtained from the \fBFiniteElement\fP which is bridged to the current \fBElement\fP that is queried\&.
.SH "Application Examples"
.PP
The characteristic "inner radius" is commonly used to find the appropriate resolution for an advection or visualization grid on which a variable is to be mapped on\&.

.PP
\fBAssignCellCharacteristicsTo()\fP can be used to:
.IP "\(bu" 2
test the shape of cells in a mesh for their suitability for a computation (aspect ratio)\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
testing how skewed the cells got by deformation
.IP "\(bu" 2
integrate cell properties in specific calculations, for instance, if a fracture is just one cell wide, the minimum height of these fracture cells may be equivalent to the fracture aperture\&.
.PP
.SH "Messages"
.PP
Firstly, the method will report an error and return, if the target property to which the cell characteristic shall be assigned to is not an cell variable\&.

.PP
Further errors may be reported if the user tries to use the characteristic area in a 3D computation, or volume in a 2D computation\&. Also, cell height and width are thus far only available in 2D\&. 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim, template< uint32_t > class CELL> void \fBcsmp::ModelSubDomain\fP< dim, CELL >::AssignNodeCoordinatesTo (const char * vector_variable)"

.PP
output coordinates to user-defined vector variable [x1,y1,z1,\&.\&.\&.xn,yn,zn] Assigns node coordinates to the supplied node variable which may be either of scalar or \fBvector<double>\fP type\&. If either the X, Y, or Z coordinate are to be assigned to a scalar variable an index from 0\&.\&.\&.dim-1 must be supplied to indicate which coordinate axis shall be mapped\&.
.SH "Input Arguments"
.PP
The name of the physical variable to which the node coordinate shall be assigned to, and, if a scalar property shall be initialized with coordinate values, an int index to indicate which coordinate axis is desired (0=X, 1=Y, 2=Z)\&.
.SH "Implementation"
.PP
Method uses the Coordinates() and X(), Y(), and Z() interfaces of the \fBNode\fP class\&.
.SH "Application Examples"
.PP
To arrive at coordinate values for a model where topography is important etc\&.
.SH "Messages"
.PP
The method will report a fatal error and terminate the computation, if the target variable is not placed on the node or if it is not of the correct type\&. 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim, template< uint32_t > class CELL> double \fBcsmp::ModelSubDomain\fP< dim, CELL >::Average (const char * prop) const"

.PP
arithmetic (number as opposed to volume weighted) average Takes the arithmetic (not cell size weighted) average of all property values of nodes, integration points, or cells that belong to the region, depending on where the target property is placed\&.

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIprop\fP The name of the property which shall be averaged\&. 
