.SS "template<uint32_t dim, template< uint32_t > class CELL> void \fBcsmp::ModelSubDomain\fP< dim, CELL >::ExtrapolateCellToNodeProperty (const char * eprop, const char * nprop, bool by_distance = \fRtrue\fP)"
Interpolates piecewise constant cell properties to the nodes\&. Since each node belongs to multiple cells their contributions to this node have to be weighted\&. This method offers two ways to do this\&.

.PP
'by-distance' is the default\&. In this approach, contributions are weighted by the inverse of the distance of each cell's barycentre from the node\&. Thus, small cells have a bigger role than big ones\&.

.PP
'default' the contributions are weighted by cell length/area/volume\&. This weighs bigger cells more than small ones\&. In many cases this is not what one wants, but it is still offered for some specific applications\&. 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim, template< uint32_t > class CELL> void \fBcsmp::ModelSubDomain\fP< dim, CELL >::ExtrapolateIntegrationPointToNodeProperty (const char * cprop, const char * nprop)"
Using the cell interpolation functions, this method extrapolates the desired integration point property to the nodes\&. Constributions of adjacent cells are averaged but no weighting by cell size or proximity of barycentre to the node is applied\&.
.SH "Input Arguments"
.PP
The names of the targeted integration point and node variables\&.

.PP
The result of the extrapolation is returned into the \fBModel\fP property storage\&.
.SH "Implementation"
.PP
The method depends on a corresponding function of the \fBFiniteElement\fP which is for all numerically integrated cell types\&. For cells with quadratic inpterpolation functions, the method uses a linear extrapolation which is justified for solution variable derivatives as they define a trilinear field on the IntegrationPoints\&.
.SH "Application Examples"
.PP
Use this for numerically integrated finite cells, in particular ones with an interpolation order greater than 1\&.
.SH "Messages"
.PP
A consistency check on variable type and placement is performed\&. 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim, template< uint32_t > class CELL> void \fBcsmp::ModelSubDomain\fP< dim, CELL >::IdentifyPerimeter ()"

.PP
distinguishes PERIMETER simplices that have at least one face on domain boundary from INTERIOR ones; calls PartitionElementVector() In order to use the region node/cell flags 'INTERIOR' or 'PERIMETER', boundary nodes and cells must be identified first\&. This is done every time when a new region is formed\&.

.PP
\fBAttention\fP
.RS 4
convention: cells are considered 'PERIMETER' cells of a \fBRegion\fP only if they have at least one edge or face at the region boundary\&. This is the case irrespective of the dimensionality of such cells\&. In the extreme case of line cells inside a volume, they are flagged boundary, if they have one node on the perimeter of the volume region\&. Another way of looking at this is to consider all cells boundary cells, that share a face with the boundary\&.

.PP
convention: If a \fBRegion\fP consists of cells of different dimensionality, the highest dimensional ones define the position of the boundary, i\&.e\&. all lower-dimensional cells that stick out of the region (and including all their nodes) are flagged 'PERIMETER'\&. Lower-dimensional cells inside a higher dimensional region are considered 'INTERIOR'\&.
.RE
.PP
.SH "Application Examples"
.PP
\fBIdentifyPerimeter()\fP is called as part of the region-forming process\&. The perimeter flagging is used to access boundaries selectively\&.

.PP
\fBTodo\fP
.RS 4
(1) Does not work for quadratic regions
.RE
.PP
.SH "Implementation"
.PP
.IP "1." 4
Perimeter cells and nodes are found looking for the absence of cell neighbors, but only the highest dimensionality cells inside the \fBRegion\fP
.IP "  \(bu" 4
all the nodes of the highest-dimensionality cells are stored in a set\&.
.PP

.IP "2." 4
Lower-dimensional cells are found that do not share all of their nodes with the highest dimensional cells\&.
.IP "  \(bu" 4
These cells nodes must be outside of the highest dimensional region\&.
.IP "  \(bu" 4
These cells and their nodes are flagged 'PERIMETER'\&.
.PP

.IP "3." 4
All lower dimensional cells that share all of their nodes with the highest dimensional cells AND have at least nodes flagged 'PERIMETER' on one edge (a single node for a line cell) will be flagged 'PERIMETER' cells\&.
.PP

.PP
It follows that all lower-dimensional mesh that 'sticks out' of a higher dimensional region has cells and nodes flagged as PERIMETER\&. 
